<h1>What Is the DOM, and Why Structure Matters</h1>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Document Object Model (DOM)</b> is the live, programmable representation of a webpage's structure. It turns elements like text, images, and buttons into objects that a programming language can change.</p>
</div>

<br>

<p>You just sorted Elements and Events into two separate categories. The DOM is the reason that distinction matters: it's the system that keeps track of every element on the page, and listens for every event a user triggers, so that code can connect the two. A click (an event) on a button (an element) only does something because the DOM is watching for it.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/72fa4c8440257c2397bfb9ebf4ed03cf-image-1786127978245.png" alt="A diagram showing a webpage next to its DOM tree. The webpage has a header with a logo and Home, About, Contact links, and a main content area with a heading, Learn about the world of coding, a paragraph of placeholder text, an image, a name input field, and a Submit button. The DOM tree branches from Document into Header and Main Content. Header branches into Title and Navigation. Main Content branches into Heading, Paragraph, Image, and Button." style="max-width: 750px; width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
</div>

<h2>Why Page Structure Matters</h2>

<p>The parts of a page aren't just about looks. They help users navigate. Screen readers use the structure of a page to move around it, a heading acts as a landmark telling a user where they are on the page, the same way it would for someone visually scanning down the screen.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Screen reader</b> is software that reads a webpage's content aloud, or converts it to braille, letting someone navigate and understand a page without seeing it.</p>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> this is the same idea from Robust in the POUR principles, a page has to work with assistive technology, not just with a mouse. Structure isn't just a coding convention, it's part of what makes a page usable at all.</p>
</div>

<p>Next, you'll see exactly what happens the moment a user acts on one of these elements, and how that change actually reaches the screen.</p>


MARKDOWN
